#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "e64a4c4d-502b-508e-b7af-b4012cd16617" :question/math-academy-id "e-14578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $24 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$\n\n- $17 \\equiv 21 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n\n- $19 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3 \\mid (24-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-12) \\div 3 = 12 \\div 3 = 4\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4 \\mid (17-21).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(17-21) \\div 4 = -4 \\div 4 = -1\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $9 \\not\\mid (19-12).$ Notice that $19-12 = 7,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "041ab8f3-ced5-5a79-b639-190850c06891" :question/math-academy-id "e-14579" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-11 \\equiv -29 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $90 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$\n\n- $-10 \\equiv 4 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $6 \\mid (-11-(-29)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-11-(-29)) \\div 6 = 18\\div 6 = 3\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true because $9 \\, | \\, (90-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(90-0) \\div 9 = 90\\div 9 = 10\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $5 \\not\\mid (-10-4).$ Notice that $-10-4 = -14,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/id #uuid "afcb17e2-d38b-5ebc-b135-f8cf68a601ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-14581" :question/problem "Given that $-7  \\equiv 17 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=6$\n\n- $n=5$\n\n- $n=4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b = -7-17= -24.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $6 \\, | \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 6 = -4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $5 \\!\\not{|} \\, (-24).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $4 \\, | \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 4 = -6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a23adb-0758-5db2-acbc-ce4a08c7bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409355} {:answer/id #uuid "45739530-0f38-5422-9dc2-6ee62cb4fa98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409364} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbe99c-cc95-5126-be17-7b1302dafd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409370} {:answer/id #uuid "ab117369-f4ee-599c-af58-4abaa57df29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409396} {:answer/id #uuid "e01ed3c3-d611-527c-a4cd-eed95fbee3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a23adb-0758-5db2-acbc-ce4a08c7bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409355} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e7547b-ae8c-5644-a80c-add06b2c90c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a4a4ae5-eba9-597a-b12c-2ce503fbc86a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103385" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $18≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d1a863f-95e0-561d-8b4e-634c0143f09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409367} {:answer/id #uuid "649b2937-4ed2-54a5-868a-a98baf189430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409374} {:answer/id #uuid "656f07f1-9270-5ce4-98e9-a7070185fd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409375} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3b6bc-bfd0-56cc-97c0-969644a1537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409380} {:answer/id #uuid "e330412d-2087-54b1-9337-003e92248756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "656f07f1-9270-5ce4-98e9-a7070185fd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409375} :answer-field/id #uuid "4165585a-237e-5905-a89b-3813ee62ad2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77d765e-3b79-5534-bec0-79f592cc8d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103387" :question/problem "Given that $-3≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-3-15=-18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷2=-9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷6=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷9=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1762b1ce-d004-57e6-ae76-4ab71ee3f948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409348} {:answer/id #uuid "96112173-be20-53cc-a11d-2eeab9cf2d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409388} {:answer/id #uuid "a580563f-2be7-54ec-9ef4-eee5fd920596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409394} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d6abbf-0176-582c-8057-a6cee55fd6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409401} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c43ee0-5593-513b-b0c8-b805b7e94411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1762b1ce-d004-57e6-ae76-4ab71ee3f948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409348} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b16f0b8-e8e0-53eb-8868-43987ff6e2b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c433443-9a9c-5d0b-84b5-bc247b100915" :question/math-academy-id "q-103390" :question/problem "Given that $29≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=29-(-16)=45.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∣45$ because $45÷5=9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $8∤45.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $9∣45$ because $45÷9=5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e65da69-f3b4-5682-a2d1-fed159b1cca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409346} {:answer/id #uuid "1a91b125-6713-5078-ad8a-07ca9759bd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409349} {:answer/id #uuid "297ef0c5-c965-585b-8851-1eee6d2a0890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409356} {:answer/id #uuid "7457c4d9-6466-5ff1-8ed5-0b76d4baa436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409377} {:answer/id #uuid "94713236-b0f8-5cb1-b15a-fa847167c239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a91b125-6713-5078-ad8a-07ca9759bd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409349} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd48921-20ab-55cd-9e00-cc0f4f65d5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a58d29a-b4cc-50fc-9d69-711422d71682" :question/math-academy-id "q-103490" :question/problem "Given that $13≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=13-(-5)=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $6∣18$ because $18÷6=3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3117461d-ae1c-5b5f-962f-5e27e86b9e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409360} {:answer/id #uuid "548199e2-9ea9-53cd-9759-d4dc756cd4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409368} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc18c0f-9ca4-5139-8f1e-43baabe1407e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409390} {:answer/id #uuid "c13f56e0-dfbd-50a7-b5cf-74bf387c8a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409402} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fa208c-47d8-594f-9b17-745f165a7c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548199e2-9ea9-53cd-9759-d4dc756cd4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409368} :answer-field/id #uuid "3917216f-e197-5a98-ab60-f1c904677e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fffccd1b-296a-5eb0-9657-b20162f80bc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-113654" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n7≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $2$\n- $10$" :question/worked-solution "To visualize congruence modulo $3,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $3$ numbers. \n\n![](images/46/46c64b99bf297e841b79bcc2e97c8a650b6ae94af8e11f2d4e85f2e8513def6c.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $3.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-1)÷3=6÷3=2\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $3∤(7-2).$ Notice that $7-2=5,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-10).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-10)÷3=-3÷3=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$1$ and $10$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9ba739-5c28-5602-a51b-7adc5826aea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409358} {:answer/id #uuid "43fe9c1c-4c13-5179-85af-285304727f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409362} {:answer/id #uuid "573c1b44-e61c-5dcd-86f3-f6c126fa80c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409369} {:answer/id #uuid "94f4cd83-45d7-50d2-989d-a0e531b12113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409386} {:answer/id #uuid "acafffee-1648-5260-91a4-404f59df8fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acafffee-1648-5260-91a4-404f59df8fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409398} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aacb179-3f6c-545f-a0cc-44920c4a8ef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff47ebb7-b97b-504b-85d7-8f24ec1bf6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-113657" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n6≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $0$\n- $1$\n- $2$" :question/worked-solution "To visualize congruence modulo $2,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $2$ numbers. \n\n![](images/93/93c588ff95bd8987e60ce2221ebef6e44c05039c13608ba35968a28c8582dd34.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $2.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-0)÷2=6÷2=3\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $2∤(6-1).$ Notice that $6-1=5,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-2).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-2)÷2=4÷2=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$0$ and $2$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c58a97-2d6f-5dcb-84fe-89a84ea632bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409345} {:answer/id #uuid "4481ac85-5137-5ba3-bdac-03a771c08079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409363} {:answer/id #uuid "a57ae88c-7aac-50d6-b17c-225ee369ed0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409393} {:answer/id #uuid "c48bbefc-49f9-5e63-ad74-522807980bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409403} {:answer/id #uuid "cd00a9ab-1308-5ad6-802b-434f4e457980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd00a9ab-1308-5ad6-802b-434f4e457980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409406} :answer-field/id #uuid "a63c404f-2cff-5183-9d79-b86b34e2f72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa9fc443-6ba1-5837-ae0a-5b71ce84408d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113658" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n11≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $3$\n- $7$" :question/worked-solution "To visualize congruence modulo $4,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $4$ numbers. \n\n![](images/06/0644cdd579eeaa9168808434167e1e007a698efeab2e5d031bf8b0159a245af4.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $4.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $4∤(11-1).$ Notice that $11-1=10,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4∣(11-3).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-3)÷4=8÷4=2\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $4∣(11-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-7)÷4=4÷4=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ and $7$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0385a2d3-ff26-5002-954b-401ff5ea7fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409343} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2570f7-17be-5eab-8bd6-c0a17a4ce8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-15$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409350} {:answer/id #uuid "8fee962f-faf7-5950-a127-1fc5a7576ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-18$ and $-9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409383} {:answer/id #uuid "a4639bc2-cf61-5bd2-b150-7aec9c833476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-18$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409391} {:answer/id #uuid "b22acfc8-7d8f-539b-9e2b-1eee88c6bbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-18,-15,$ and $-9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2570f7-17be-5eab-8bd6-c0a17a4ce8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-15$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409350} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6dff25-36e4-5065-9c16-d64430a17dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97d3bcb5-c2f9-55ac-a2c8-208f62457770" :question/math-academy-id "q-212516" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n-3≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $-18$\n- $-15$\n- $-9$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-18)).$ Notice that $-3-(-18)=15,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $12∣(-3-(-15)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-3-(-15))÷12=12÷12=1\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-9)).$ Notice that $-3-(-9)=6,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$-15$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6491d9-1d90-5386-995a-284889bdd49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409351} {:answer/id #uuid "2b860813-d4b1-5fef-b506-6ffcc8c555af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409357} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4173a3-d315-5fb7-9f00-3bd771b4750f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409359} {:answer/id #uuid "956c7dd0-6d81-5565-ae83-7839bf085246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409387} {:answer/id #uuid "b68067c2-b8d7-5f62-b933-7366f89a68bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956c7dd0-6d81-5565-ae83-7839bf085246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7ce273-1469-5ebe-853b-6ac76cc6e24b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc725849-ec72-5565-a2a3-c18e7bade536" :question/math-academy-id "q-76784" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409342} {:answer/id #uuid "05752e2f-49d8-5029-8640-38d4fb2c4302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409344} {:answer/id #uuid "79a9d6c6-5a61-5865-93e5-90b3c4aec498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409379} {:answer/id #uuid "d45d7b30-c602-5bbb-b327-69ce48a4fb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409408} {:answer/id #uuid "e8143651-dbed-5f90-b38d-d9e1c91175c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409342} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27defb4-bb86-5b12-8d1d-2f258f68bd34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "665aa776-ec33-596f-ae58-1e8a076473e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76952" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484f4bca-d64b-544b-b87d-1bacf4c35986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409365} {:answer/id #uuid "48f7b955-4755-5e56-9f1c-71e139699c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409366} {:answer/id #uuid "6e577ca3-4903-5b1b-a794-47a45d1f0c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409376} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc3ad2-783a-5d36-ba03-cfd9ee2be154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409407} {:answer/id #uuid "e318448a-9322-5d40-bf1b-76ebf9029ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc3ad2-783a-5d36-ba03-cfd9ee2be154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409407} :answer-field/id #uuid "a610aa87-901f-5a33-b3da-57a6452cd19a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42227076-6750-5c0c-9261-152604f91699" :question/math-academy-id "q-76953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $17≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $23≡-10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $9∤(17-2).$ Notice that $17-2=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $5∤(23-(-10)).$ Notice that $23-(-10)=33,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(33-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(33-12)÷7=21÷7=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "621c44d6-93dc-5220-ad30-48313fff1042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409372} {:answer/id #uuid "86dcace0-8e17-596b-b607-813bfd8841f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409382} {:answer/id #uuid "93692f71-aa41-54d8-9db3-7599da38199e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409384} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c696b6-e407-551e-9c85-750416b8ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409404} {:answer/id #uuid "e828b90d-adc9-50db-9dff-69261bedddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e828b90d-adc9-50db-9dff-69261bedddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409415} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90945b6-ae5d-503f-bb6d-c321c3cb1593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae89ee49-3f2d-51e4-a2f6-ac3e918a674e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76958" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $12≡36\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n- $-9≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $21≡45\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $2∣(12-36).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(12-36)÷2=-24÷2=-12\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $4∤(-9-18).$ Notice that $-9-18=-27,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $8\\,|\\,(21-45).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(21-45)÷8=-24÷8=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf0a248-db5e-5429-b19f-8baec1eccfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409389} {:answer/id #uuid "a563bf47-26ea-5012-bc40-ac94dd282a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409392} {:answer/id #uuid "ab09181d-d458-54be-b834-2e63ecb92ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409395} {:answer/id #uuid "ac52b52e-9b59-55a6-aee5-bdcca2c19458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409397} {:answer/id #uuid "ecaef5e2-552f-53c9-8afa-1e06d0245904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab09181d-d458-54be-b834-2e63ecb92ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409395} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c0b011b-f8f7-5378-93a8-0f3125f9e17e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b17c24c5-e750-5126-8785-6318018ca783" :question/math-academy-id "q-76959" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $-7≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $6≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $3∤(-5-11).$ Notice that $-5-11=-16,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $7\\,|\\,(-7-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-0)÷7=-7÷7=-7\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $9∤(6-25).$ Notice that $6-25=-19,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a59032-d154-5cd1-9f78-1e77115fceb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409347} {:answer/id #uuid "1eba9774-714c-562d-9d13-0447aea71267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409352} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce80dc4-d91c-5754-ac3f-33f63cb1cb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409361} {:answer/id #uuid "789e77ae-772c-5d06-a9df-5fa5f38d83c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409378} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed8879a-6f30-55b0-9f22-b4a53cb96a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789e77ae-772c-5d06-a9df-5fa5f38d83c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409378} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b10380e-960d-5141-bbf4-f7b3eb5af3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ce0c9ed-8f49-5909-b44d-93bd60aa3e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76964" :question/problem "Given that $26≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=9$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=26-8=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $8∤18.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe10586-c932-5ec9-a06b-8cf711a3abc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409353} {:answer/id #uuid "283ae9e3-5cbe-51dc-93f9-66705b94a4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409354} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbb7e64-ba7b-569a-a95a-1beb7a13f9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409371} {:answer/id #uuid "62eb87b3-63f2-54d1-be4f-335750bbeb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409373} {:answer/id #uuid "dc534a4c-c7e6-54fe-be3a-e9ddb8d5d053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fbb7e64-ba7b-569a-a95a-1beb7a13f9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409371} :answer-field/id #uuid "3562348b-86e5-5e46-9766-895a1ac9633f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80a88f89-ac3c-581b-83f4-0bc0a136d398" :question/math-academy-id "q-76972" :question/problem "Given that $8≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=5$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=8-23=-15.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷3=-5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $4∤(-15).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $5∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷5=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
